The grammar accepts `+` after `dyn` and `some` (the only place `+` survives) and no rule says what a value of two interfaces is:

interface Shape:
    fn area() -> float

shapes: Array[owns dyn Shape + Debug] = [owns Square(side=2.0)]

fn show(s: dyn Shape + Debug):
    println(s.area(), f"{s!r}")        # which table does the call go through? `dyn I` has one table pointer; a value of two interfaces has no layout in the text, so a package cannot lay it out or write the call

fn visible(xs: Span[int]) -> some Iterator[Item = int] + Clone:
    return xs.iter().copy_each().filter(owns fn(x) => x > 0)

An implementer cannot answer: the layout of `dyn A + B` (one table pointer, two, or a combined table), nor whether `+` here is meant when TYP-17 refuses it everywhere else.

```
@noalloc
fn fall(ref mut ps: Array[Particle], dt: float, gravity: float, k: float):
    pull: fn(ref mut Particle) = fn(ref mut p): p.vy -= gravity * dt
    drag: fn(ref mut Particle) = fn(ref mut p) copy of k: p.vy *= k * dt
    for ref mut p in ps:
        pull(ref mut p)
        drag(ref mut p)
```
The one-word rule is now fixed (`copy of k`, read only, lives in the state word). What is not fixed is where the addresses of real variables live. `[CLO-10]` makes "where the real variables it uses live" part of the state and says it "costs nothing beyond their addresses"; `pull` uses two real variables (`gravity`, `dt`), so two addresses, and a `{code, state}` value has one state word. An implementer who stores the two addresses in a block makes `pull`'s line `Alloc`, `E4001` in `@noalloc fall`; one who stores the address of the frame that holds them (one word) does not. `drag` is worse: its one-word capture takes the state word and it also uses the real `dt`, so nothing says whether that is a block. Two compilers decide `E4001` differently on the same program, which `[PHIL-13]` and `[PHIL-2]` forbid. The finder's question stands in this narrowed form.

