@noalloc
fn start(log: thread_shared Log) -> JoinHandle[()]:
    return thread.spawn(owns fn() thread_shared log: log.push("a"))

The function value's state is one word, the `thread_shared` owner. `[CLO-10]`: "a state of one word or less that the function's body never writes is kept in the state word itself ... an owner or a link it only reads through (a body writes its state when ... the name ... has a method called on it that changes it, worked out as `[EFF-24]` works out what a function changes)". `Log.push` changes `this.lines` under `this.lock` (Part XI's `Log`), so by the letter the body "has a method called on it that changes it", the state is written, the state lives in a block, the line carries `Alloc`, and `start` is `E4001`. But the reason the rule gives for the block ("so that a call through a copy of the two words changes the state the holder keeps") does not hold here: the word is a pointer to a counted block, the change is made in that block, and every copy of the two words points at it; nothing is gained by the allocation, which is paid at every `thread.spawn(owns fn() thread_shared log: ...)` and every `fn() shares mut count: count.n += 1`. The same reading would make `[CLO-18]` demand `ref mut h` to call such a handler, although the change is the shared object's under `[RC-7]`, not the holder's.

fn first_word(line: StrView) -> StrView:
    toks = line.split_whitespace()
    for t in toks:
        return t
    return ""

